Questions for Reflection

1. What are the basic rules of XML you applied in this lab?

The basic rules I applied were properly opening and closing all tags, 
using a single root element, correctly nesting the elements, 
and making sure the XML document follows the correct structure and syntax.

2. Why does XML need a single root element?

XML needs a single root element because it acts as the main container of all the data in the document.
It keeps the XML document organized and allows the data to have a clear hierarchical structure.

3. How does XML ensure interoperability?

XML ensures interoperability by using a standard and platform-independent format for storing and exchanging data.
Different applications, systems, and programming languages can read and process XML data.

Short Reflection Paragraph

In this activity, I learned how to create and validate a simple XML document. 
I learned the importance of using proper opening and closing tags, correct nesting, and a
single root element. Validating the XML helped me understand how to identify and correct errors in the document.
Overall, the activity helped me understand how XML can organize and exchange data between different systems.
